      {
        "thread_id": "TH2",
        "thread_goal": "Design and finalize the standardized predictive maintenance workflow.",
        "messages": [
          {
            "msg_id": "M19",
            "speaker_id": 36422,
            "reply_to": null,
            "text": "Hi team, following up on our alignment, I've drafted the new standardized workflow in A2, which covers T2 and T3. The goal is to have a clear process from alert generation to resolution. I'd appreciate your feedback, especially on the escalation paths defined in section 3. Does the proposed triage process make sense?",
            "mentions": [
              560411,
              53841,
              237177,
              18383
            ],
            "artifact_refs": [
              "A2",
              "T2",
              "T3"
            ],
            "decision_refs": [],
            "reactions": []
          },
          {
            "msg_id": "M20",
            "speaker_id": 560411,
            "reply_to": "M19",
            "text": "Thanks for sharing this so quickly. The flow is very clear. I have one suggestion regarding the escalation from a Level 1 (Operator) alert to Level 2 (Maintenance Tech). The current draft says 'if unresolved in 30 mins'. I'd propose we add a condition based on alert severity. A high-severity alert, like a critical temperature spike, should be escalated immediately, regardless of the 30-minute window. This will prevent potential damage.",
            "mentions": [
              36422
            ],
            "artifact_refs": [
              "A2"
            ],
            "decision_refs": [],
            "reactions": [
              {
                "emoji": "clap",
                "count": 1
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "msg_id": "M21",
            "speaker_id": 53841,
            "reply_to": "M19",
            "text": "This looks like a solid improvement over our current ad-hoc process. My main question is about communication. How do we ensure the production supervisor is kept in the loop when an asset is being investigated by maintenance? We can't afford to have them discover a machine is offline by surprise.",
            "mentions": [],
            "artifact_refs": [],
            "decision_refs": [],
            "reactions": []
          },
          {
            "msg_id": "M22",
            "speaker_id": 237177,
            "reply_to": "M19",
            "text": "When does the garage get involved in this process?",
            "mentions": [],
            "artifact_refs": [],
            "decision_refs": [],
            "reactions": []
          },
          {
            "msg_id": "M23",
            "speaker_id": 36422,
            "reply_to": "M20",
            "text": "Excellent point, that's a critical refinement. I'll add severity-based immediate escalation. That's a much more robust approach.",
            "mentions": [
              560411
            ],
            "artifact_refs": [],
            "decision_refs": [],
            "reactions": [
              {
                "emoji": "+1",
                "count": 1
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "msg_id": "M24",
            "speaker_id": 36422,
            "reply_to": "M21",
            "text": "Good question. I'll add a step for the Maintenance Tech to notify the Production Supervisor via radio call as soon as they begin investigating on the floor. We can formalize that in the procedure.",
            "mentions": [
              53841
            ],
            "artifact_refs": [],
            "decision_refs": [],
            "reactions": []
          },
          {
            "msg_id": "M25",
            "speaker_id": 36422,
            "reply_to": "M22",
            "text": "The garage is the Level 3 escalation, for issues that require specialized tooling or major component replacement that can't be done on the line. It's detailed in section 3.2 of A2.",
            "mentions": [
              237177
            ],
            "artifact_refs": [
              "A2"
            ],
            "decision_refs": [],
            "reactions": []
          },
          {
            "msg_id": "M26",
            "speaker_id": 55217,
            "reply_to": "M19",
            "text": "Who decides if it's a Level 3 issue?",
            "mentions": [],
            "artifact_refs": [],
            "decision_refs": [],
            "reactions": []
          },
          {
            "msg_id": "M27",
            "speaker_id": 560411,
            "reply_to": "M26",
            "text": "The process doc suggests the on-shift Maintenance Lead makes that call, which seems right. They have the best context on available resources and repair complexity.",
            "mentions": [
              55217
            ],
            "artifact_refs": [],
            "decision_refs": [],
            "reactions": []
          },
          {
            "msg_id": "M28",
            "speaker_id": 18383,
            "reply_to": "M19",
            "text": "I've read the document. It seems ok. Is there a process for when an alert might be a sensor failure instead of a machine failure?",
            "mentions": [],
            "artifact_refs": [],
            "decision_refs": [],
            "reactions": []
          },
          {
            "msg_id": "M29",
            "speaker_id": 36422,
            "reply_to": "M28",
            "text": "That's a good point. The L2 Maintenance Tech's first step should be to validate the reading. I'll make that explicit in the checklist within A2. Thanks for catching that.",
            "mentions": [
              18383
            ],
            "artifact_refs": [
              "A2"
            ],
            "decision_refs": [],
            "reactions": []
          },
          {
            "msg_id": "M30",
            "speaker_id": 36422,
            "reply_to": "M29",
            "text": "Okay, with these refinements, let's lock down the roles. DECISION: For alerts, Line Operators are responsible for L1 triage, and on-shift Maintenance Techs are responsible for L2 investigation and response.",
            "mentions": [],
            "artifact_refs": [],
            "decision_refs": [
              "D4"
            ],
            "reactions": [
              {
                "emoji": "+1",
                "count": 3
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "msg_id": "M31",
            "speaker_id": 53841,
            "reply_to": "M30",
            "text": "Works for me.",
            "mentions": [],
            "artifact_refs": [],
            "decision_refs": [],
            "reactions": []
          },
          {
            "msg_id": "M32",
            "speaker_id": 237177,
            "reply_to": "M30",
            "text": "Understood.",
            "mentions": [],
            "artifact_refs": [],
            "decision_refs": [],
            "reactions": []
          },
          {
            "msg_id": "M33",
            "speaker_id": 36422,
            "reply_to": "M30",
            "text": "Great. And for the overall process: DECISION: The standardized predictive maintenance process and escalation paths documented in A2 are approved, pending the discussed revisions.",
            "mentions": [],
